import ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.ParityCheckMatrix

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.18 — `H G = 0` for a generator/parity-check pair

*(N&C §10.4.1, p. 447.)*

Parity check matrix H and generator matrix G of a linear code satisfy HG=0.

## What the exercise asks, and what is proved

N&C §10.4.1 present a linear code two ways: by a **generator matrix** `G` (an `n × k` matrix), whose
code is the span of its columns — the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x`
(`Matrix.range_mulVecLin`) — and by a **parity-check matrix** `H` (an `(n − k) × n` matrix), whose
code is its kernel `{x | H *ᵥ x = 0}` (eq. (10.57)). When `H` and `G` describe the *same* code,
every column of `G` is a codeword, hence annihilated by `H`, so `H G = 0`.

The formalisation is in `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/ParityCheckMatrix.lean`, alongside the
classical-coding development (`GeneratorMatrix`, `LinearCode`, `DualCode`, `RepetitionCode`).

## Declarations

All in `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/ParityCheckMatrix.lean`, in the `Matrix` namespace, over
an
arbitrary commutative semiring `R` (N&C's binary code is `R = ZMod 2`), with `H : Matrix l m R` and
`G : Matrix m n R` (so `H * G : Matrix l n R`):

* `Matrix.mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_le_ker` — the underlying linear-algebra fact: if the code
  generated by `G` is contained in the code checked by `H`
  (`LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin ≤ LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin`), then `H * G = 0`. Only containment is
  needed — the columns of `G` are among the words `H` annihilates.
* `Matrix.mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_eq_ker` — **the exercise itself**: when `H` and `G` present
  the *same* code (`LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin = LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin`), `H * G = 0`.

## Faithfulness

* **Statement.** "Generator matrix `G` and parity check matrix `H` for the same code" is captured by
  the library's standard code conventions — the code generated by `G` is the column span
  `range G.mulVecLin` (as in `GeneratorMatrix`), the code checked by `H` is `ker H.mulVecLin` (N&C
  eq. (10.57), as in `RepetitionCode.repetitionParityCheck_ker`) — and "the same code" is exactly
  their equality. The conclusion `H * G = 0` is stated verbatim. Nothing is weakened.
* **Only containment is used.** The proof needs only that `G`'s columns are codewords, i.e.
  `range G.mulVecLin ≤ ker H.mulVecLin`; the `_le` lemma records this stronger (more reusable) form,
  and the exercise's "same code" equality is the immediate special case. This is a strengthening of
  the reusable lemma, not a weakening of the exercise.
* **Generality.** The coefficients range over an arbitrary commutative semiring, so N&C's `ZMod 2`
  is a special case and (as N&C remark in Exercise 10.19) the result extends beyond `ZMod 2`.
-/
